    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B01",
      "act": "BACKGROUND",
      "narration_text": "Sound is a wave of pressure. Add the same wave flipped upside down, and the two cancel: every high meets a low. That flipped copy is called anti-noise. Here's an engine hum at the ear, the anti-noise my simulated headphone made for it, and what's left.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "choreographed",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "AncWaves",
          "props": {
            "title": "Add the Wave, Upside Down",
            "sparkLine": "Noise plus anti-noise: almost nothing.",
            "eyebrow": "HUMANITARIANS AI · AUDIO CONCEPTS",
            "cues": {
              "wave": 0.0361,
              "flip": 0.1883,
              "cancel": 0.2885,
              "anti": 0.5449,
              "hum": 0.647,
              "made": 0.7592,
              "left": 0.9535
            },
            "durationSeconds": 16.64
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B01.mp4",
            "at": "2026-10-23T23:53:21.083931+00:00"
          }
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 17,
      "actual_duration_s": 16.64,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B01.mp3",
      "render_duration_s": 16.666666666666668,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media\\B01.mp4",
        "filled_by": "remotion:AncWaves",
        "at": "2026-10-23T20:40:37"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B02",
      "act": "MECHANISM",
      "narration_text": "A microphone on the outside of the ear cup hears the noise first. A chip turns it into anti-noise and plays it through the speaker. Many headphones also have a microphone inside, listening to what's left. My simulated one uses it to keep adjusting its filter, forty-eight thousand times a second. The noise takes eighty-three microseconds to reach the ear, and the electronics take a hundred and twenty-five. So the chip has to predict a little of the future.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "choreographed",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "AncSystem",
          "props": {
            "title": "Listen, Flip, Check, Adjust",
            "sparkLine": "The chip has to stay ahead of the noise.",
            "eyebrow": "HUMANITARIANS AI · AUDIO CONCEPTS",
            "cues": {
              "outside": 0.0191,
              "chip": 0.1411,
              "speaker": 0.2301,
              "inside": 0.3267,
              "adjust": 0.5149,
              "rate": 0.5823,
              "noise": 0.6738,
              "elec": 0.806,
              "predict": 0.9446
            },
            "durationSeconds": 26.22
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B02.mp4",
            "at": "2026-10-23T23:56:51.569756+00:00"
          }
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 26,
      "actual_duration_s": 26.22,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B02.mp3",
      "render_duration_s": 26.25,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media\\B02.mp4",
        "filled_by": "remotion:AncSystem",
        "at": "2026-10-23T20:40:37"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B03",
      "act": "MATH",
      "narration_text": "Being a little late matters more at high pitch. A timing error of a tenth of a millisecond is a tiny slice of a hundred-hertz wave: three point six degrees, and the hum still drops twenty-four decibels. At two thousand hertz, the same delay is seventy-two degrees, and the anti-noise now adds to the noise. Past sixty degrees, there's no cancellation at all.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "choreographed",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "AncPhase",
          "props": {
            "title": "Late Matters More at High Pitch",
            "sparkLine": "Same delay, bigger mistake at high pitch.",
            "eyebrow": "HUMANITARIANS AI · AUDIO CONCEPTS",
            "cues": {
              "late": 0.0059,
              "error": 0.1634,
              "hundred": 0.2704,
              "drops": 0.4441,
              "two": 0.5541,
              "adds": 0.7858,
              "sixty": 0.8601
            },
            "durationSeconds": 22.44
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B03.mp4",
            "at": "2026-10-24T00:00:23.939426+00:00"
          }
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 24,
      "actual_duration_s": 22.44,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B03.mp3",
      "render_duration_s": 22.458333333333332,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media\\B03.mp4",
        "filled_by": "remotion:AncPhase",
        "at": "2026-10-23T20:40:37"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B04",
      "act": "MEASURED",
      "narration_text": "I ran four kinds of noise through my simulated headphone. Engine hum: twenty-five decibels quieter. Low rumble: thirty-one. Voices: only eleven. Clatter: none at all, in fact slightly louder. Steady, low sounds are predictable, so the chip can stay ahead of them. Sudden sounds aren't. Listen: the hum without it, then with it.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "choreographed",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "AncTypes",
          "props": {
            "title": "What It Cancels, and What It Can't",
            "sparkLine": "Steady and low: gone. Sudden: still there.",
            "eyebrow": "HUMANITARIANS AI · AUDIO CONCEPTS",
            "listen": [
              {
                "label": "ENGINE HUM: ANC OFF, THEN ON",
                "from": 0.8174,
                "to": 0.9696
              }
            ],
            "cues": {
